  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 133 AM CDT Tue Sep 15 2026\n\nA potent surface low pressure system centered over southern \nManitoba early this morning is tracking northeast toward Hudson \nBay, pulling a cold front eastward across Minnesota while a warm \nfront lifts northward into Iowa. Aloft, a strong 50 to 60 knot \nlow-level jet directed into northeast Wisconsin has generated \nwidespread showers and scattered embedded thunderstorms early \nthis morning. Latest radar imagery and surface observations \nconfirm a swath of 1 to 3 inches of rain, with localized reports \nexceeding 3 inches, extending from Portage and Waushara counties \nnortheastward into Outagamie and Brown counties. Although local \ncounty dispatch reported no widespread flooding overnight, radar \nestimates and private weather stations indicated rainfall rates \nexceeding 6-hour flash flood guidance, maintaining a localized \nurban and small stream flooding risk early this morning. Will\nmaintain the Flood Advisory for east-central WI until expiration.\nAs the low- level jet veers eastward, the heaviest precipitation \nis shifting south and east of the area.\n\nLight to moderate rain today through Wednesday Night...\nLight to moderate showers along with isolate storms will linger \nthrough mid to late morning until the cold front sweeps across the\narea. Additional rainfall amounts up to 0.50 inches will be \npossible across east- central and northeast WI. Strong boundary \nlayer mixing behind the front will yield clearing skies and breezy\nwest winds this afternoon, with frequent gusts of 20 to 30 mph \nexpected before winds diminish rapidly near sunset.\n\nSurface high pressure building across the northern Great Lakes \nwill bring dry and tranquil weather for tonight and Wednesday. \nRadiational cooling beneath clear to partly cloudy skies and light\nwinds will promote the formation of patchy ground fog late \ntonight into early Wednesday morning, particularly across far \nnorthern Wisconsin. High temperatures Wednesday will be \nseasonable, reaching the upper 60s to low 70s. As the frontal zone\nslowly returns north Wednesday night, elevated warm advection \nwill bring a 30 to 50 percent chance of light showers to central \nand east- central Wisconsin. However, moisture availability is \nmodest and elevated instability reaches only 100 to 200 J/kg, \nkeeping the probability of rainfall exceeding 0.50 inches under 10\nto 20 percent in the southern Fox Valley, with no heavy rain \nimpacts anticipated.\n\nWidespread rain late Friday into Saturday...\nA more dynamic weather system is forecast to impact north-central\nand northeast Wisconsin late Friday into Saturday as a shortwave\nejects from the western United States trough. Supported by a 40 \nto 50 knot low-level jet and precipitable water values rising to \naround 1.70 inches (roughly 95 percent of seasonal normal), \nwidespread showers and embedded thunderstorms will spread across \nthe region. Model guidance indicates a 40 to 50 percent \nprobability for 24-hour rainfall amounts to exceed 1 inch across \nmuch of the area from Friday night through Saturday morning. \nElevated instability remains limited with Most Unstable CAPE \ngenerally under 500 J/kg, keeping the severe thunderstorm risk \nlow. Forecast predictability decreases markedly Sunday into Monday\ndepending on how quickly the main western trough moves eastward, \nbut lingering mid-level energy will maintain a 20 to 30 percent \nchance of showers through Sunday.",
